Time taken by train to cover first $5 \mathrm{~km}=\frac{\text { Distance }}{\text { Speed }}=\frac{5}{30}=\frac{1}{6} \mathrm{hr}$
Time taken by train to cover next $15 \mathrm{~km}=\frac{15}{45}=\frac{1}{3} \mathrm{hr}$.
$\begin{aligned}
& \text { Average speed }=\frac{5+15}{\frac{1}{6}+\frac{1}{3}} \\
& =\frac{20}{\frac{1+2}{6}}=\frac{120}{3}=40 \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{hr}
\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned}
& (1+2 x) d y-(1-2 y) d x=0 \Rightarrow(1+2 x) d y=(1-2 y) d x \\
& \Rightarrow \frac{d y}{1-2 y}=\frac{d x}{1+2 x}
\end{aligned}$
Integrating both sides, $\int \frac{d y}{1-2 y}=\int \frac{d x}{1+2 x}\begin{aligned}
& \Rightarrow \frac{-1}{2} \log (1-2 \mathrm{y})=\frac{1}{2} \log (1+2 \mathrm{x})+\frac{1}{2} \log \mathrm{c} \\
& \Rightarrow \log (1-2 \mathrm{y})+\log (1+2 \mathrm{x})=\log \mathrm{c} \\
& \Rightarrow(1+2 \mathrm{x})(1-2 \mathrm{y})=\mathrm{c} \\
& \Rightarrow 1+2 \mathrm{x}-2 \mathrm{y}-4 \mathrm{xy}=\mathrm{c} \\
& \Rightarrow 2 \mathrm{x}-2 \mathrm{y}-4 \mathrm{xy}=\mathrm{c} \\
& \Rightarrow \mathrm{x}-\mathrm{y}-2 \mathrm{xy}=\mathrm{c}
\end{aligned}$

$\int \frac{d x}{2^x-1}=\int \frac{d x}{\frac{1}{2^{-x}}}=\int \frac{2^{-x}}{1-2^{-x}} \cdot d x$
Let $1-2^{-\mathrm{x}}=\mathrm{t} \Rightarrow 2^{-\mathrm{x}} \cdot \log 2=\frac{\mathrm{dt}}{\mathrm{dx}}\begin{aligned}
& \Rightarrow 2^{-\mathrm{x}}=\frac{1}{\log 2} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{dt}}{\mathrm{dx}} \Rightarrow 2^{-\mathrm{x}} \cdot \mathrm{dx}=\frac{\mathrm{dt}}{\log 2} \\
& \therefore \int \frac{2^{-\mathrm{x}}}{1-2^{-\mathrm{x}}} \mathrm{dx}=\frac{1}{\log 2} \int \frac{\mathrm{dt}}{\mathrm{t}}=\frac{1}{\log 2}(\log \mathrm{t})+\mathrm{c} \\
& =\frac{1}{\log 2}\left(\log \left(1-2^{-\mathrm{x}}\right)\right)+\mathrm{c}
\end{aligned}$

$\tan ^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)+\tan ^{-1}\left(\frac{3}{5}\right)$
We know, $\tan ^{-1} x+\tan ^{-1} y=\tan ^{-1}\left(\frac{x+y}{1-x y}\right)$
So, $\tan ^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)+\tan ^{-1}\left(\frac{3}{5}\right)=\tan ^{-1}\left(\frac{\frac{1}{4}+\frac{3}{5}}{1-\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)\left(\frac{3}{5}\right)}\right)\begin{aligned}
& =\tan ^{-1}\left(\frac{\frac{5+12}{20}}{1-\frac{3}{20}}\right) \\
& =\tan ^{-1}\left(\frac{\frac{17}{20}}{\frac{17}{20}}\right)=\tan ^{-1}(1)=\frac{\pi}{4}
\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned}
& A=\left[\begin{array}{ll}
1 & 2 \\
2 & 3
\end{array}\right] \\
& A^2=\left[\begin{array}{ll}
1 & 2 \\
2 & 3
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 2 \\
2 & 3
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1+4 & 2+6 \\
2+6 & 4+9
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
5 & 8 \\
8 & 13
\end{array}\right] \\
& \mathrm{A}^2-\mathrm{kA}-\mathrm{I}_2=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
5 & 8 \\
8 & 13
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mathrm{k} & 2 \mathrm{k} \\
2 \mathrm{k} & 3 \mathrm{k}
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{array}\right] \\
& =\left[\begin{array}{cc}
5-\mathrm{k}-1 & 8-2 \mathrm{k}-0 \\
8-2 \mathrm{k}-0 & 13-3 \mathrm{k}-1
\end{array}\right] \\
& =\left[\begin{array}{cc}
4-\mathrm{k} & 8-2 \mathrm{k} \\
8-2 \mathrm{k} & 12-3 \mathrm{k}
\end{array}\right]
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned}
& \text { Given, } \mathrm{A}^2-\mathrm{kA}-\mathrm{I}_2=0 \\
& \therefore 4-\mathrm{k}=0 \Rightarrow \mathrm{k}=4
\end{aligned}$

$\mathrm{x}, \frac{3}{2}, \mathrm{z} \text { are in A.P }$
If $a, b, c$ are in A.P $2 b=a+c\begin{aligned}
& \therefore 2\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)=\mathrm{x}+\mathrm{z} \\
& \Rightarrow 3=\mathrm{x}+\mathrm{z}...(1) \\
& \mathrm{x}, 3, \mathrm{z} \text { are in G.P. }
\end{aligned}$
If $a, b, c$ are in G.P. $b^2=\mathrm{ac}\begin{aligned}
& \therefore 3^2=\mathrm{xz} \\
& \Rightarrow 9=\mathrm{xz}...(2)
\end{aligned}$
If $x, 6, z$ are in H.P. $\frac{2}{6}=\frac{1}{x}+\frac{1}{z}\begin{aligned}
& \qquad\left(\because \frac{2}{b}=\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{c} \text {, if } a, b, c \text { are is H.P. }\right) \\
& \Rightarrow \frac{1}{3}=\frac{z+x}{x z}=\frac{3}{9}=\frac{1}{3}(\operatorname{from}(1)(2)) \\
& \text { L.H.S. }=\text { R.H.S. }
\end{aligned}$
